---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const modules = import.meta.glob('./manifestos/*.md', { eager: true });
const manifestos = Object.values(modules)
  .map((m: any) => m.frontmatter)
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => a.number - b.number);
---

<Base title="The ORES Manifestos">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="inner">
      <h1>Principles we can be held to.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Ten positions on how software gets built here — written down so they can be argued with,
        cited in a review, and checked by a tool, instead of being inferred from a diff by whoever
        happens to be reading it.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        Most of these exist because we got the opposite wrong first. They are stated as rules
        because a principle you have to re-derive under deadline pressure is not a principle.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="manifestos">
    <div class="inner">
      <h2>The manifestos</h2>
      <p class="section-note">Each one is short, and each one says what it costs.</p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        {manifestos.map((m: any) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/manifestos/${m.slug}`}>
            <span class="num">{String(m.number).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3>{m.title}</h3>
            <p>{m.tagline}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <div class="inner">
      <h2>Why write these down</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A workspace of this shape — many organizations, the same repository patterns repeated
          across all of them, a lot of work done by automation — fails in a particular way. Not
          through any single bad decision, but through a hundred locally reasonable ones that
          nobody wrote down and nobody can therefore review.
        </p>
        <p>
          An unstated convention is enforced by whoever remembers it. A stated one can be checked
          by a program, cited in a pull request, disagreed with in the open, and changed on
          purpose. That is the whole argument.
        </p>
        <p>
          Several of these are deliberately expensive. Two independently authored contract sources
          cost more than one. Gates that refuse to guess block builds that a laxer gate would have
          let through. That is the trade being made, on purpose, with the reasons written down
          where the next person can weigh them.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the operational side of building and running the services themselves, see
          <a href="https://22-factor-apps.github.io" rel="noopener">The Twenty-Two-Factor App</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
